<start_of_turn>model
","To find the polarization in the electrochemical cell, we first need to determine the cell potential (E_cell) using the Nernst equation. The cell potential can be calculated using the standard reduction potentials of the two half-reactions:

Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu (E° = +0.342 V)
Ag⁺ + e⁻ → Ag (E° = +0.7996 V)

The overall cell reaction is:

Cu²⁺ + e⁻ → Cu and Ag⁺ + e⁻ → Ag

Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu (E° = +0.342 V)
Ag⁺ + e⁻ → Ag (E° = +0.7996 V)

Subtract the reduction potentials of the anode (Cu²⁺/Cu) from the reduction potential of the cathode (Ag⁺/Ag) to get the cell potential:

E_cell = E°(cathode) - E°(anode)
E_cell = (+0.7996 V) - (+0.342 V)
E_cell = +0.4576 V

Now, we need to find the cell potential (E_cell) under non-standard conditions using the Nernst equation:

E_cell = E° - (RT/nF) * ln(Q)

Where:
R = gas constant (8.314 J/mol·K)
T = temperature (298 K for standard conditions)
n = number of electrons transferred (1 in this case)
F = Faraday's constant (96485 C/mol)
Q = reaction quotient

The reaction quotient (Q) for the cell reaction is:

Q = [Cu²⁺] / [Ag⁺]

E_cell = 0.4576 - (8.314 * 298 / (1 * 96485)) * ln(0.2 / 0.1)
E_cell = 0.4576 - 0.0252 * ln(2)
E_cell = 0.4576 - 0.0252 * 0.693
E_cell = 0.4576 - 0.0175
E_cell = 0.4401 V

The polarization is the difference between the standard cell potential and the actual cell potential under non-standard conditions:

Polarization = E° - E_cell
Polarization = 0.4576 V - 0.4401 V
Polarization = 0.0175 V

