","To find the molecular weight of the unknown solute, we use the boiling point elevation formula:

\[
\Delta T_b = K_b \cdot m
\]

Where:
- \(\Delta T_b\) is the boiling point elevation
- \(K_b\) is the ebullioscopic constant
- \(m\) is the molality of the solution

First, we need to find the ebullioscopic constant \(K_b\) for CCl₄. We can use the data from the naphthalene solution to find \(K_b\):

\[
\Delta T_b = 0.402 \, \text{°C}
\]
\[
0.5126 \, \text{g} \, \text{naphthalene} = \frac{0.5126}{128.17} \, \text{mol} = 0.0040 \, \text{mol}
\]
\[
m = \frac{0.0040 \, \text{mol}}{0.050 \, \text{kg}} = 0.080 \, \text{mol/kg}
\]
\[
0.402 = K_b \times 0.080
\]
\[
K_b = \frac{0.402}{0.080} = 5.03 \, \text{°C/mol}
\]

