In a Cartesian coordinate system, what is the curl of a vector field \(\mathbf{V}(x, y)\) that depends only on \(x\) and \(y\)? Express the result in terms of its components.
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The curl of a vector field \(\mathbf{V}(x, y) = \langle u_x(x, y), u_y(x, y), 0 \rangle\) is given by:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \begin{vmatrix}
\mathbf{i} & \mathbf{j} & \mathbf{k} \\
\frac{\partial}{\partial x} & \frac{\partial}{\partial y} & \frac{\partial}{\partial z} \\
u_x(x, y) & u_y(x, y) & 0
\endendvmatrix}
\]
Expanding the determinant, we get:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \left( \frac{\partial}{\partial y} \cdot 0 - \frac{\partial}{\partial z} \cdot u_y(x, y) \right) \mathbf{i} - \left( \frac{\partial}{\partial x} \cdot 0 - \frac{\partial}{\partial z} \cdot u_x(x, y) \right) \mathbf{j} + \left( \frac{\partial}{\partial x} \cdot u_y(x, y) - \frac{\partial}{\partial y} \cdot u_x(x, y) \right) \mathbf{k}
\]
Since the \(z\)-component of \(\mathbf{V}\) is zero, the second and third terms vanish. Thus, the cross-product simplifies to:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \left( \frac{\partial}{\partial y} \cdot 0 \right) \mathbf{i} - \left( \frac{\partial}{\partial x} \cdot 0 \right) \mathbf{j} + \left( \frac{\partial}{\partial x} \cdot u_y(x, y) - \frac{\partial}{\partial y} \cdot u_x(x, y) \right) \mathbf{k}
\]
Simplifying further, we get:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \boxed{\left( \frac{\partial u_y}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial u_y}{\partial y} \right) \mathbf{k}}
\]
