","The decay length (\(\lambda_d\)) of an evanescent wave is given by:
\[
\lambda_d = \frac{\lambda}{2\pi \Delta n}
\]
where:
- \(\lambda\) is the wavelength of the evanescent wave,
- \(\Delta n\) is the refractive difference between the material of the cladding and the medium of the evanescent wave.

Here, the cladding is Ga\(_{0.2}\)Al\(_{08}\)As, and the evanescent wave is in GaAs. The decay length is given by the reference document as:
\[
\lambda_d = \frac{\lambda}{2\pi (n_{\text{cl}} - n_f)}
\]
where \(n_{\text{cl}}\) is the refractive index of the cladding (Ga\(_{0.2}\)Al\(_{0.8}\)As) and \(n_f\) is the refractive index of the evanescent wave medium.

Given:
- \(\lambda = 1 \, \mu m = 1000 \, \mu m = 1000000 \, \mu m\)
- \(n_{\text{eff}} - n_f \sim 0.3\). Here, \(n_{\text{eff}}\) is the refractive index of the core (GaAs), and \(n_f\) is the refractive index of the medium of the evanescent wave.

Thus, the difference between the refractive index of the core and the evanescent wave medium is \(\Delta n = n_{\text{eff}} - n_f = 0.3\).

Substituting the values:
\[
\lambda_d = \frac{1000 \, \mu m}{2\pi \times 0.3} = \frac{1000}{0.6 \pi} \, \mu m \approx 524.2 \, \mu m
\]

Thus, the decay length of the evanescent wave is \(\boxed{524.2 \, \mu m}\).",ground_truth_similarity,"# Task Description
