In an elastic scattering process where the electron mass is negligible, let the initial and final four-momenta of the electron be \( P_i = (E_i/c, \mathbf{p}_i) \) and \( P_f = (E_f/c, \mathbf{p}_f) \), respectively. Derive an expression for the squared four-momentum transfer \( Q^2 \).<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","The squared four-momentum transfer \( Q^2 \) is defined as the difference between the final and initial four-momenta:
\[
Q^2 = (P_f - P_i)^2
\]
Substituting the definitions of \( P_i \) and \( P_f \):
\[
Q^2 = (P_f - P_i)^2 = (P_f - P_i)^2 = (P_f - P_i)^2
\]
Expanding the square:
\[
Q^2 = (P_f - P_i)^2 = (P_f - P_i)^2 = (P_f - P_i)^2
\]
Using the definition of the dot product for four-momentum:
\[
(P_f - P_i)^2 = P_f \cdot P_f - 2 P_f \cdot P_i + P_i \cdot P_i
\]
Substituting the components:
\[
Q^2 = \left( \frac{E_f}{c} \right)^2 - 2 \left( \frac{E_f}{c} \right) \left( \frac{E_i}{c} \right) \cos \theta + \left( \frac{E_i}{c} \right)^2
